We can approach this problem by considering the total number of ways to choose three vertices from the six vertices of the hexagon. This is a combination problem, and the formula for combinations is C(n, k) = n! / [k!(n-k)!], where n is the total number of items, k is the number of items to choose, and "!" denotes factorial.
answer If we use all six vertices to form triangles, we would actually have a convex hexagon, and one of those would be a hexagon itself. But we are seeking triangles whose vertices split the hexagon into parts. If we draw all the diagonals of a hexagon and split it into triangles, we can then count the number of triangles.
[asy]size(130);
import olympiad;
draw((dir(10)--dir(-70)--dir(-190)));
draw((dir(30)--dir(-50)--dir(-150)));
draw((dir(30)--dir(-50)));
draw((dir(-280)--dir(-150)--dir(-110)));
draw((dir(30)--dir(320),dashed));
draw((dir(-10)--dir(-170),dashed));
draw((dir(-170)--dir(-150),dashed));
draw((dir(-150)--dir(-50),dashed));
label("6",(dir(-90)+1.2*(dir(150)+dir(90)))/2,s);[/asy]
